
.TH SYNC 1 "October 2026" "sync" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sync \- synchronize cached writes to persistent storage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sync
.RB [ \-df ]
.RI [ FILE ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sync
synchronizes cached filesystem writes to persistent storage.
.PP
If no files are specified,
.B sync
requests synchronization of all mounted filesystems using
the
.BR sync (2)
system call.
.PP
If one or more files are specified,
.B sync
synchronizes each file individually using
.BR fsync (2).
This includes file data and associated metadata.
.PP
With the
.B \-d
option, only file data and metadata required for subsequent
data retrieval are synchronized using
.BR fdatasync (2).
.PP
With the
.B \-f
option, the entire filesystem containing each specified file
is synchronized using
.BR syncfs (2).
If no files are specified, all filesystems are synchronized.
.PP
The
.B \-d
and
.B \-f
options are mutually exclusive.
The
.B \-d
option requires at least one filename.
.PP
Files are processed independently.
If an operation fails, an error is reported and processing
continues with the remaining files.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-data
Synchronize file data without unnecessary metadata.
Uses
.BR fdatasync (2).
Requires at least one filename.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file-system
Synchronize the entire filesystem containing each specified
file using
.BR syncfs (2).
Without filenames, synchronize all filesystems.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display usage information and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Synchronize all filesystems:
.PP
.RS
.B sync
.RE
.PP
Synchronize a single file, including its metadata:
.PP
.RS
.B sync /var/log/messages
.RE
.PP
Synchronize only file data and required metadata:
.PP
.RS
.B sync -d /var/log/messages
.RE
.PP
Synchronize the filesystem containing a directory:
.PP
.RS
.B sync -f /home
.RE
.PP
Synchronize multiple files:
.PP
.RS
.B sync file1.txt file2.txt
.RE
.PP
Synchronize the filesystems containing multiple paths:
.PP
.RS
.B sync -f /home /var
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option or option combination was specified,
a required filename was missing, or an error occurred
while opening, synchronizing, or closing a file.
.SH NOTES
The
.B sync
command requests that the kernel synchronize cached writes
with the underlying storage devices.
The exact persistence guarantees depend on the filesystem,
storage hardware, and kernel implementation.
.PP
When filenames are specified, the implementation opens
them with
.B O_NONBLOCK
and attempts read-only access first, falling back to
write-only access if necessary.
.PP
The command does not recursively traverse directories.
When
.B \-f
is used, synchronization applies to the entire filesystem,
regardless of whether the specified pathname refers to
a file or directory.
.SH STANDARDS
The basic
.B sync
functionality conforms to POSIX.
The
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.B \-\-help
options are extensions.
.PP
This implementation targets Linux and uses the
Linux-specific
.BR syncfs (2)
system call for filesystem synchronization.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsync (2),
.BR fdatasync (2),
.BR sync (2),
.BR syncfs (2),
.BR mount (8)
